1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:18,411
This is ECE 358, video 16 b. The topic is address aggregation, also called supernetting. It covers slides 59 to 63 of chapters 4 and 5. It uses longest prefix matching from video 16 a.

2
00:00:18,861 --> 00:00:37,037
After this video, you can split the block of an I S P into blocks for organizations. You can explain why hierarchical addressing makes routing tables small. And you can aggregate address blocks into summary addresses. The question bank and slide 63 ask this.

3
00:00:37,487 --> 00:01:00,527
Before aggregation, slide 60 shows where a network gets its addresses. It gets a portion of the address space of its provider I S P. The I S P gets its block from ICANN. Here, the block of the I S P is 200 dot 23 dot 16 dot 0, slash 20. The 20 network bits are purple.

4
00:01:00,977 --> 00:01:11,814
The host part has 32 minus 20, which is 12 bits. So the block has 2 to the power 12 addresses. That is 4096 addresses.

5
00:01:12,264 --> 00:01:45,794
The I S P splits its block into 8 blocks, one for each organization. 8 is 2 to the power 3. So 3 more bits, in orange, pick the block. Organization 0 gets 0 0 0. That is 200 dot 23 dot 16 dot 0, slash 23. Organization 1 gets 0 0 1, which is 200 dot 23 dot 18 dot 0. Organization 2 gets 200 dot 23 dot 20 dot 0.

6
00:01:46,244 --> 00:01:59,535
The pattern continues to organization 7, with bits 1 1 1. That is 200 dot 23 dot 30 dot 0, slash 23. The third byte goes up by 2 each time.

7
00:01:59,985 --> 00:02:14,961
Each block has 2 to the power 12, divided by 8, which is 2 to the power 9, or 512 addresses. 9 host bits give a prefix of 32 minus 9, which is slash 23.

8
00:02:15,411 --> 00:02:36,957
Slide 61 shows why this allocation helps. Eight organizations connect through router R 1 of Fly-By-Night I S P. R 1 tells router R 2 in the Internet which addresses it can reach. Without aggregation, R 1 advertises 8 routes, one prefix for each organization.

9
00:02:37,407 --> 00:02:55,071
With aggregation, R 1 advertises only 1 route: send me anything with addresses that begin with 200 dot 23 dot 16 dot 0, slash 20. The single slash 20 prefix covers all 8 slash 23 blocks.

10
00:02:55,521 --> 00:03:09,900
Where is the hierarchy? The slash 20 block of the I S P is the parent. The 8 slash 23 blocks of the organizations are its children. Routers outside the I S P see only the parent.

11
00:03:10,350 --> 00:03:21,550
So hierarchical addressing allows efficient advertisement of routing information. Other routers keep fewer entries, and fewer advertisements cross the network.

12
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:37,765
Slide 62 shows address aggregation, also called supernetting. Router A has 4 entries: 192 dot 168 dot 0, dot 1, dot 2 and dot 3, each slash 24.

13
00:03:38,215 --> 00:03:53,469
Write the four addresses in binary. The first 22 bits are the same in all four. Only bits 23 and 24 change. They take all four values: 0 0, 0 1, 1 0 and 1 1.

14
00:03:53,919 --> 00:04:07,209
So the four blocks together form one block with a 22 bit prefix: 192 dot 168 dot 0 dot 0, slash 22. This is the summary address.

15
00:04:07,659 --> 00:04:35,051
Router A advertises: I can reach 192 dot 168 dot 0 dot 0, slash 22. Router B keeps 1 entry instead of 4. Check the size: 4 blocks of 256 addresses is 1024, which is 2 to the power 10. A slash 22 prefix leaves 10 host bits. So the summary covers exactly the four blocks, and nothing else.

16
00:04:35,501 --> 00:04:45,421
Slide 63 does the same for the 8 organizations. In binary, the first 20 bits, in green, are the same in all 8 blocks.

17
00:04:45,871 --> 00:04:53,359
Bits 21 to 23, in orange, take all 8 values, from 0 0 0 to 1 1 1.

18
00:04:53,809 --> 00:05:14,673
So the summary address is 200 dot 23 dot 16 dot 0, slash 20. This is the block of the I S P. Check: 8 blocks of 512 addresses is 4096, which is 2 to the power 12. A slash 20 leaves 12 host bits. Exact.

19
00:05:15,123 --> 00:05:39,870
The method has three steps. One: write the blocks in binary. Two: find the common prefix. Three: check that the summary holds exactly the given blocks, and no other addresses. If it holds extra addresses, split the blocks into groups. Slide 63 says that a possible final exam question is: given a few address blocks, find their summary addresses.

20
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:50,773
Here is the question on slide 63. Take the 8 networks, but remove network 3. Compute the summary addresses. Pause the video and try it.

21
00:05:51,223 --> 00:06:12,621
Draw the third byte as a number line from 16 to 31. Each slash 23 block covers 2 values. Organization 3 is 200 dot 23 dot 22 dot 0, so values 22 and 23 are missing. A summary must not cover them. So one slash 20 is not possible.

22
00:06:13,071 --> 00:06:29,028
Start at the left. Organizations 0 and 1 cover 16 to 19. That is 4 values, which starts at a multiple of 4. So it is one block: 200 dot 23 dot 16 dot 0, slash 22.

23
00:06:29,478 --> 00:06:43,046
Organization 2 covers 20 and 21. It cannot join 22 and 23. So it stays alone: 200 dot 23 dot 20 dot 0, slash 23.

24
00:06:43,496 --> 00:06:58,643
Organizations 4 to 7 cover 24 to 31. That is 8 values, which starts at a multiple of 8. So it is one block: 200 dot 23 dot 24 dot 0, slash 21.

25
00:06:59,093 --> 00:07:17,439
So the answer is three summary addresses: slash 22, slash 23, and slash 21. Seven entries become three. Rule: a block of 2 to the power k values must start at a multiple of 2 to the power k. I checked this answer with Python.

26
00:07:17,889 --> 00:07:33,079
Question 66: aggregate six destination networks. All six use interface 1. The question bank has no published answers, so this solution uses the method of the slides. Pause the video and try it.

27
00:07:33,529 --> 00:07:41,017
Only the third byte changes. Draw it as a number line from 0 to 7. Values 1 and 4 are missing.

28
00:07:41,467 --> 00:07:53,051
Value 0 cannot join 1, because 1 is missing. So 192 dot 168 dot 0 dot 0 slash 24 stays alone.

29
00:07:53,501 --> 00:08:05,767
Values 2 and 3 make a block of 2 that starts at a multiple of 2. So they become 192 dot 168 dot 2 dot 0, slash 23.

30
00:08:06,217 --> 00:08:27,999
Values 5, 6 and 7 are 3 values. 3 is not a power of 2, so they cannot be one block. Value 5 stays alone, slash 24. Values 6 and 7 start at a multiple of 2, so they become 192 dot 168 dot 6 dot 0, slash 23.

31
00:08:28,449 --> 00:09:01,872
Why not one entry, 192 dot 168 dot 0 dot 0 slash 21? That block covers values 0 to 7, so it also covers networks 1 and 4. A packet to 192 dot 168 dot 4 dot 9 would match it and go out on interface 1. But that network is not in the table. Slide 63 asks for summary addresses, plural, when one net is missing. So a summary must not add addresses.

32
00:09:02,322 --> 00:09:15,271
So the answer is four entries, all on interface 1: slash 24, slash 23, slash 24, slash 23. Six entries become four. I checked this with Python.

33
00:09:15,721 --> 00:09:27,988
Question 67 has the same six networks. But now networks 5, 6 and 7 use interface 3. Compare it with question 66. Pause the video and try it.

34
00:09:28,438 --> 00:09:42,987
The first group is 0, 2 and 3, on interface 1. As before, 0 stays alone, and 2 and 3 become 192 dot 168 dot 2 dot 0, slash 23.

35
00:09:43,437 --> 00:09:58,584
The second group is 5, 6 and 7, on interface 3. As before, 5 stays alone, and 6 and 7 become 192 dot 168 dot 6 dot 0, slash 23.

36
00:09:59,034 --> 00:10:17,125
The difference: an aggregate entry has one interface. So you can only aggregate entries with the same interface. Here, the missing networks 1 and 4 already split the groups. So the blocks are the same as in question 66. Only the interfaces differ.

37
00:10:17,575 --> 00:10:44,881
The answer: 192 dot 168 dot 0 dot 0 slash 24 and 192 dot 168 dot 2 dot 0 slash 23 on interface 1. 192 dot 168 dot 5 dot 0 slash 24 and 192 dot 168 dot 6 dot 0 slash 23 on interface 3.

38
00:10:45,331 --> 00:11:01,011
To recap. An I S P block with prefix p, split into 2 to the power k blocks, gives blocks with prefix p plus k. Aggregation advertises one summary route instead of many. Routers keep fewer entries.

39
00:11:01,461 --> 00:11:19,296
To aggregate, write the blocks in binary, find the common prefix, and check that the summary holds exactly the given blocks. A block of 2 to the power k values starts at a multiple of 2 to the power k. Only entries with the same interface can merge.

40
00:11:19,746 --> 00:11:24,759
The next video covers NAT, autonomous systems, and the basics of routing.
